# EVERY OPERATION THAT NAMES A POSITION IN A PROCESS NOTATION. One row per filed
# `pm:Operation/foreignId`: the notation, the id inside it, and the operation here that claims it.
#
# ⭐⭐⭐ THE THIRD `pm:ForeignId`, AND THE LAST ONE WITHOUT A FILE. The model carries exactly
#    three references out of a document: an `asrt:Part`, an `asrt:Elimination/between`, and this.
#    The part has `composition/part_references.sqlc` and a rule that verifies it resolves; the
#    elimination has `eliminations/references.sqlc` and an observation, because the schema
#    licenses a `between` that lands nowhere. ⚠️ TWO THINGS OF ONE TYPE OWE THE SAME APPARATUS,
#    and an asymmetry between them is visible from `ls` without reading a line of SQL.
#
# ⛔⛔ AND NO RULE MAY JUDGE IT, WHICH IS WHY NOTHING PULLED IT INTO VIEW. This tree grows by
#   rules, and a field no rule reads is under no pressure to exist. `pm:ForeignId`'s own
#   annotation refuses the check outright: a notation plus an id names a position in ONE file
#   that no authority publishes, so a receiver sent looking for `Task_17` in a normative list
#   finds nothing there and reads it as a defect in the list. There is nothing to resolve
#   against, so `checks/unresolved_part.sqlc`'s shape copied across would accuse every filing.
#   ⭐ The answer is the same one `between` got: report, never assert.
#
# ⭐⭐ AND THE OTHER ARM IS NOW A FACT RATHER THAN A SILENCE. `notationPosition` is REQUIRED
#    and takes `pm:StatedForeignId`, so an operation that names no position says WHY: `none` is
#    in no notation and somebody looked, `unmeasured` is a notation exists and nobody located
#    this operation in it, `notApplicable` is this filing has no notation at all. This relation
#    is the stated arm; `queries/observations/17-notation-references.sqlc` reports both, because
#    the population is the finding and the restriction alone would hide it.
#
# ⚠️ THE NOTATION IS A LANGUAGE, NOT A DOCUMENT, AND THAT IS THE DIFFERENCE FROM A PART. A part
#   names another FILING and `filing_identity` resolves it. This names the OMG BPMN MODEL URI,
#   and the id names a node in whichever BPMN document travels beside the filing. So the pair
#   identifies a position RELATIVE to a document nobody here holds, which is exactly the
#   property that makes the crossing cost the receiver nothing.
-- pm:Operation/pm:notationPosition, the stated arm: a notation plus an id.
SELECT o.filing, o.label, o.foreign_notation, o.foreign_id
FROM ( :compose(entries/operations.sqlc) ) o
WHERE o.foreign_notation IS NOT NULL
